  geom:"osm",   // OpenStreetMap channel clipped to the park boundary — don't re-snap
  osmName:"Nonvianuk River",
  parkRegsSrc:"katmai",
  parkRegs:"An <b>Alaska sport fishing licence</b> is required of all nonresidents 16 and over and most residents 16 to 59; you may also need a harvest record card and/or king salmon stamp. Fishing falls under ADF&amp;G's Bristol Bay, Kodiak/Aleutian and Lower Cook Inlet management areas. <b>Keep at least 50 yards between yourself and all bears, and stop fishing when a bear is within 50 yards of you.</b><br><br><b>Alaska 2026 regulations</b><br><b>ALAGNAK RIVER DRAINAGE (Unless further restricted below. See map above):</b><br>• Use of helicopters for transporting anglers and sport- caught fish is prohibited.<br><b>Chum, coho, pink, and sockeye salmon (combo):</b><br>• 5 per day, 5 in possession, only 3 per day, 3 in possession may be coho salmon.<br>• King salmon: See general regulations and limits on page 10.<br><b>Rainbow trout:</b><br>• June 8-October 31: Catch-and-release fishing only. All rainbow trout caught must be released immediately.<br>• November 1-April 9: 5 per day, 5 in possession, must be less than 18 inches long.<br>• Alagnak River Drainage - all flowing waters of the Alagnak River drainage, including lake waters within ½ mile of all inlet and outlet streams:<br>• June 8-April 9: Open to sport fishing.<br>• Only unbaited, single-hook, artificial lures or flies may be used.<br><i>— ADF&amp;G 2026 Southwest regulations, p. 15</i><br><br>Everything not covered above falls under the <b>Bristol Bay general regulations</b> — ADF&amp;G 2026 Southwest booklet, p. 10.",
