  id:"wt_lowerdesmoines", name:"Lower Des Moines River Water Trail", color:"#5b6b2f",
  state:"IA", gauges:["wtDesMoinesKeosauqua"], primaryGauge:"wtDesMoinesKeosauqua",
  waterTrail:{name:"Lower Des Moines River Water Trail", src:"Iowa DNR", designated:"2007-07-14", counties:["Van Buren","Davis"], miles:46.3, url:"https://www.iowadnr.gov/media/8889/download?inline"},
  geom:"iawt",   // the Iowa DNR's water-trail line (iowa/watertrails) — never snapped or refined
  goodFlow:null, // TODO: set {min:___,max:___} (CFS) for YOUR good-flow range on this river
  blurb:"The Iowa DNR's Lower Des Moines River Water Trail: 46.3 designated miles in Van Buren and Davis Counties, designated in July 2007 (extended in July 2016). Lowland forest, rock outcrops and old river towns from Eldon past Lacey-Keosauqua State Park to Farmington. It is a meandered stream: the bed, sandbars and banks below the high-water mark are public, and sandbar camping is allowed. Releases from Lake Red Rock upstream can change the level quickly, and the invasive silver carp in this reach leap when a boat goes by and can hit paddlers. The DNR maps 10 access sites along it, 1 of them fishing accesses rather than ramps. Of its 9 DNR segments, 5 Beginner, 2 Intermediate, 1 Advanced and 1 not yet rated. The DNR's dam layer shows 1 dam on it (Bonaparte Lock & Dam). (per Iowa DNR, 2026-10)",
