  osmName:"Clinch River",
  parkRegsSrc:"vdwr",
  parkRegs:"<b>Creel and length limits: Largemouth, Smallmouth Bass</b> (p. 22)<br><i>Clinch River–within the boundaries of Scott, Wise, Russell or Tazewell counties</i><br>• No largemouth or smallmouth bass less than 20 inches, only 1 per day longer than 20 inches<br><br><b>Statewide creel (harvest) and length limits</b> (pp. 22, 23, 24, 25)<br>The tables that follow give statewide creel (harvest) and length limits for major sport fish and exceptions for major rivers and lakes. Regulations for many smaller lakes and boat access areas are posted on site, and posted regulations are in effect (see “OTHER USES” under Department-owned Lakes, Ponds, Streams or Boat Access Sites, page 14). For smaller lakes, information can be found online at: www.virginiawildlife.gov/fishing. Creel limits are per person per day.<br>• Largemouth, Smallmouth Bass: 5 per day in the aggregate (combined); No statewide length limits (p. 22)<br>• Striped Bass — Landlocked Striped Bass and Landlocked Striped Bass X White Bass Hybrids: 4 per day in the aggregate; No fish less than 20 inches (p. 23)<br>• Striped Bass — Anadromous (coastal) striped bass above the fall line in all coastal rivers of the Chesapeake Bay and Crane Lake (City of Suffolk): Creel and length limits shall be as set by the Virginia Marine Resources Commission for recreational fishing in tidal waters (p. 23)<br>• Striped Bass — Striped bass in the Meherrin, Nottoway, Blackwater (Chowan Drainage), North Landing and Northwest rivers and their tributaries plus Back Bay: No possession (p. 23)<br>• Walleye, Saugeye: 5 per day in the aggregate; No walleye or saugeye less than 18 inches (p. 23)<br>• Sauger: 2 per day; No statewide length limits (p. 23)<br>• White Bass: 5 per day; No statewide length limits (p. 23)<br>• Chain Pickerel and Northern Pike: 5 per day; No statewide length limits (p. 23)<br>• Muskellunge: 1 per day; No muskellunge less than 40 inches (p. 23)<br>• Bluegill (bream) and other sunfish excluding Crappie, Rock Bass (redeye) and Roanoke Bass: 50 per day in the aggregate (combined); No statewide length limits (p. 24)<br>• Crappie (black or white): 25 per day in the aggregate (combined); No statewide length limits (p. 24)<br>• Rock Bass (redeye): 25 per day in the aggregate with Roanoke bass; No statewide length limits (p. 24)<br>• Roanoke Bass: 25 per day in the aggregate with rock bass; No statewide length limits (p. 24)<br>• Perch (yellow and white): No statewide daily limit; No statewide length limit (p. 24)<br>• Trout: 6 per day; 7 inch minimum size (p. 24)<br>• Catfish — Channel, White, and Flathead: 20 per day; No length limits (p. 24)<br>• Catfish — Blue: 20 per day; No statewide length limits (p. 24)<br>• Hickory Shad — Above and below the fall line in all coastal rivers of the Chesapeake Bay and their tributaries: Creel and length limits shall be the same as those set by the Virginia Marine Resources Comm. in tidal rivers (p. 25)<br>• Hickory Shad — Meherrin River below Emporia Dam, Nottoway River, Blackwater River (Chowan Drainage), North Landing and Northwest rivers, and their tributaries plus Back Bay: 10 per day No length limits (p. 25)<br>• Anadromous (coastal) Alewife and Blueback Herring — Above and below the fall line in all coastal rivers of the Chesapeake Bay and their tributaries: Creel and length limits shall be the same as those set by the Virginia Marine Resources Comm. for these species in tidal rivers (p. 25)<br>• Other native or naturalized nongame fish: 20 per day No length limits (p. 25)<br>• Other native or naturalized nongame fish — Threatened and endangered species: No possession (p. 25)<br>• Grass Carp: No statewide daily limit No statewide length limit (p. 25)<br>• Longnose Gar: July 1 to April 14: 5 per day April 15 to June 30: 1 per day No statewide length limits (p. 25)<br>• Bowfin: July 1 to April 14: 5 per day April 15 to June 30: 1 per day No statewide length limits (p. 25)<br>• Northern Snakehead: No statewide daily limit No statewide length limit (p. 25)<br>• Non-native (Exotic) Fish: See page 28 (p. 25)",
