  parkRegs:"<details><summary>This river's own rules — 5 reaches, tap to read</summary><b>AROOSTOOK RIVER, T8 R8 WELS to Fort Fairfield (North Zone)</b> (p. 8)<br>General fishing laws apply, except:<br>– From the junction of Munsungan Stream and Millinocket Stream in T8 R8 WELS downstream to the blue markers at the Oxbow Plantation/T9 R7 WELS town line (includes tributaries upstream to the first road crossing, except Mooseleuk Stream): ALO, S-19. Only 1 brook trout may exceed 12 inches. From August 16 - September 30: ALO. Total daily bag limit on brook trout: 1 fish.<br>– From the blue markers at the Oxbow Plantation/T9 R7 WELS town line downstream to the Caribou Dam, in Caribou: S-11. From August 16 - September 30: ALO. Total daily bag limit on brook trout: 1 fish.<br>– From the Caribou Dam, in Caribou, downstream to the Maine/New Brunswick border (includes tributaries upstream to the first road crossing): ALO, S-19. Only 1 brook trout may exceed 12 inches. From August 16 - September 30: ALO. Total daily bag limit on brook trout: 1 fish.<br>– From the Caribou Dam, in Caribou, downstream to and including Otter Brook, including all tributaries in this section upstream to the first road crossing intersecting each tributary: Children 12 years of age or younger may fish from shore with a single-baited hook and line (Title 12, subsection 12458).<br>– From Hockenhull Brook in Fort Fairfield downstream to and including Pattee Brook, including all tributaries in this section upstream to the first road crossing intersecting the tributary: Children 12 years of age or younger may fish from shore with a single-baited hook and line (Title 12, subsection 12458).<br><br><i>The codes above, from the lawbook's Season and Special Law Codes</i> (inside front cover flap):<br>• ALO: Artificial lures only.<br>• S-11: The use or possession of live baitfish/live smelts is permitted.<br>• S-19: Daily limit on brook trout (includes splake and Arctic charr): 2 fish.</details><br><br><b>Standard rule (North Zone): General Fishing Laws for Inland Waters</b> (inside front cover flap)<br>• Terminal gear, North Zone:<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Use or possession of live fish as bait is PROHIBITED. The use of all other legal forms of bait (including worms and dead baitfish/smelts), artificial lures, and artificial flies is PERMITTED.<br>• Seasons, rivers, streams, brooks (North and South Zones):<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;From Apr 1 to Aug 15: Open to open water fishing.<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;From Aug 16 to Sep 30: Open to open water fishing using artificial lures or flies only. General law length and bag limits apply, except daily bag limit one landlocked salmon and one brook trout.<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;From Oct 1 to Mar 31: Closed to all fishing.<br>• Daily bag &amp; possession limits by species · length limits, rivers, streams, brooks:<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Brook Trout (includes Splake &amp; Arctic charr): 5 fish · 6 inches minimum<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Brown Trout: 2 fish · 6\" min &amp; 25\" max<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Rainbow Trout: 2 fish · 6 inches minimum<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Landlocked Salmon: 2 fish · 14\" min &amp; 25\" max<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Togue (Lake Trout): 2 fish · 18 inches minimum<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Bass (Largemouth &amp; Smallmouth): North Zone: Unlimited · None<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Whitefish: 3 fish · None<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Smelts: 2 quarts · None<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Sea-run Atlantic Salmon, Redfin Pickerel, Swamp Darter: Endangered or Threatened Species - No Fishing Permitted<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Striped Bass, Shad, River Herring, American Eel, Sturgeon: See page A-1<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Inland species not listed above: Unlimited · None",
