  troutClass:"mo_blue", reachClass:["mo_blue","mo_white","mo_white"], troutSource:"mdc",
  parkRegsSrc:"mdc",
  parkRegs:"<details><summary>This river's own rules — 6 reaches, tap to read</summary><i>Trout Fishing (p. 24):</i> Missouri has several types of trout fishing areas, each with its own special rules. In addition to a fishing permit, you will need a trout permit to possess trout on all waters outside of the trout parks and a daily tag while fishing in the trout parks. Unless otherwise indicated, the season is open all year.<br><br><b>Blue Ribbon Trout Area: Current River</b><sup>1,2</sup> (p. 26)<br>Directions and total miles: River and its tributaries from lower boundary of Montauk State Park to the river crossing on Dent County Road 6510/Shannon County Big Creek Road at Cedargrove — 9 miles<br>• County: Dent and Shannon · Length Limit: At least 18\" · Daily Limit: 1 · Authorized Lures: Artificial lures and flies<br><br><b>White Ribbon Trout Area: Current River</b><sup>1,2</sup> (p. 26)<br>Directions and total miles: Downstream from the river crossing on Dent County Road 6510/Shannon County Big Creek Road at Cedargrove — 7.7 miles<br>• County: Dent and Shannon · Length Limit: Rainbows: none; Browns: at least 15\" · Daily Limit: 4 · Authorized Lures: All<br><br><i>Notes to the table (p. 27):</i><br><sup>1</sup> The use of porous-soled waders is prohibited. See Page 24.<br><sup>2</sup> While on any waters with length limits, all trout you possess must be kept with head, tail, and skin intact.<br><br><i>Porous-Soled Waders (p. 24):</i> To prevent the spread of an invasive algae called didymo, the use of shoes, boots, or waders with porous soles of felt, matted, or woven fibrous material is prohibited at all trout parks and where specified on pages 26–35.<br><br><i>What a Blue Ribbon Trout Area is (p. 26):</i> Blue Ribbon Trout Areas include parts of large, cold rivers with excellent trout habitat and smaller streams that support naturally reproducing rainbow trout. Harvest is limited to maintain the maximum density of adult trout, create excellent catch-and-release fishing, and provide the occasional chance to harvest a trophy. These areas on the Current River and North Fork of the White River are stocked with brown trout, and the Eleven Point River is stocked with rainbows.<br><br><i>What a White Ribbon Trout Area is (p. 26):</i> White Ribbon Trout Areas are coldwater streams capable of supporting trout populations year-round. All receive periodic stockings of rainbow trout, and some also receive brown trout. They provide great opportunities for catching and harvesting trout and the occasional chance to harvest a large trout.<br><br><b>Current River</b> (p. 31)<br><i>Mainstem and its tributaries:</i><br>• Walleye and sauger — 18\" minimum length limit<br><i>In Dent, Texas, and Shannon counties:</i><br>• The use of porous-soled waders is prohibited. See Page 24.<br><i>From Montauk State Park to the river crossing on Dent County Road 6510/Shannon County Big Creek Road at Cedargrove:</i><br>• Blue Ribbon Trout Area — See Page 26 for special regulations.<br>• Only flies and artificial lures may be used, and soft plastic baits and natural and scented baits are prohibited when fishing for any species.<br>• Gigging, bowfishing, and using an atlatl are prohibited, and fish taken by these methods may not be possessed on these waters or their banks.<br><i>From the river crossing on Dent County Road 6510/Shannon County Big Creek Road at Cedargrove to the Arkansas line:</i><br>• Nongame fish — The daily limit may include no more than 5 hog suckers.<br><br><b>Ozark National Scenic Riverways</b> (National Park Service, <a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/ozar/learn/management/fishing.htm\">fishing page</a>, last updated November 4, 2024; fetched 2026-10-23)<br>• A valid fishing permit is required to fish in the park. A trout permit is also required when fishing for trout in addition to a regular fishing permit.<br>• Fishing permits are not sold within the park.<br>• Specific regulations for fishing on the Current and Jacks Fork in the Ozark National Scenic Riverways can be found on the Missouri Department of Conservation's website …<br><b>Superintendent's Compendium</b> (<a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/ozar/learn/management/ozar-superintendent-s-compendium.htm\">NPS</a>)<br>• Springs – All activities and entry (to include but not limited to fishing, wading, swimming, remote control devices and vessels) into all spring pools and spring branches on federally owned land within ONSR boundaries is prohibited.<br><i>The compendium lists three exceptions to the spring closure: boating access on Big Spring Branch and two vehicle crossings (Cave Spring, Schafer Spring).</i></details><br><br><b>Statewide: Game Fish Seasons and Limits</b> (p. 20)<br>• Trout · Open Season: All year · Daily Limit: 4<sup>1</sup> · Length Limit: Rainbow: none<sup>1</sup>; Brown: 15\" minimum in streams<br>• Black bass (largemouth, smallmouth, spotted/Kentucky) · Daily Limit: 6<sup>1</sup><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;– From impoundments · Open Season: All year · Length Limit: None<sup>1</sup><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;– From most streams south of the Missouri River<sup>2</sup> · Open Season: May 23, 2026–Feb. 28, 2027 · Length Limit: 12\" minimum<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;– From other streams<sup>2</sup> · Open Season: All year · Length Limit: 12\" minimum<br><sup>1</sup> Applies on all waters, with the exception of those with more specific regulations found on pages 24–35 and on areas that are posted with special regulations.<br><sup>2</sup> Black bass fishing and possession is open year-round on the Mississippi River, all waters north of the south bank of the Missouri River, the St. Francis River downstream from Wappapello Dam, and on streams in that portion of southeast Missouri bounded by a line from Cape Girardeau following Missouri highways 74 and 25, U.S. highways 60, 67, and 160, and the west bank of the Little Black River to the Arkansas state line. In the rest of the state, black bass from streams may not be possessed from March 1–May 22, 2026. Only catch-and-release fishing for black bass is allowed during this time in this area of the state.<br><br><b>Statewide: Nongame Fish Seasons and Limits</b> (p. 21)<br>• Gig, atlatl · Season: Sept. 15–Feb. 15 · Time: Sunrise to midnight · Location: Streams and impounded waters<br><i>Daily limits, and every other method, are in that table on p. 21.</i>",
  goodFlow:null, // TODO: set {min:___,max:___} (CFS) for YOUR good-flow range on this river
  blurb:"The National Park Service says the Current and Jacks Fork rivers “have some of the best sport fishing opportunities in the country for both Smallmouth Bass, Rainbow and Brown Trout” (Ozark National Scenic Riverways). MDC manages the Current River as trout water: a Blue Ribbon Trout Area (river and its tributaries from lower boundary of Montauk State Park to the river crossing on Dent County Road 6510/Shannon County Big Creek Road at Cedargrove, 9 miles, Dent and Shannon counties); a White Ribbon Trout Area (downstream from the river crossing on Dent County Road 6510/Shannon County Big Creek Road at Cedargrove, 7.7 miles, Dent and Shannon counties) (2026 Summary, pp. 26–27). MDC says its Blue Ribbon areas on the Current and the North Fork of the White River “are stocked with brown trout” (p. 26).",
  fish:"smallmouth bass, rainbow and brown trout (NPS, of the Current and Jacks Fork); brown trout (stocked, Blue Ribbon reach); rainbow trout, periodically stocked; some White Ribbon streams also get brown trout (p. 26)",
