  parkRegsSrc:"mdc",
  parkRegs:"<b>Jacks Fork River</b> (p. 32)<br><i>From Highway 17 to the Current River:</i><br>• Smallmouth bass — 15\" minimum length limit. The daily limit of 6 black bass may include only 1 smallmouth bass.<br><br><b>Smallmouth Bass Special Management Area: Jacks Fork River</b> (MDC Fishing Atlas)<br>Shannon and Texas counties. From the Hwy. 17 bridge to the Current River.<br>• Smallmouth Bass: Minimum length limit of 15\" and daily limit of 1.<br><a href=\"https://mdc.mo.gov/fishing/regulations/special-waterbody-regulations/jacks-fork-river\">MDC's page for this water</a><br><br><b>Ozark National Scenic Riverways</b> (National Park Service, <a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/ozar/learn/management/fishing.htm\">fishing page</a>, last updated November 4, 2024; fetched 2026-10-23)<br>• A valid fishing permit is required to fish in the park. A trout permit is also required when fishing for trout in addition to a regular fishing permit.<br>• Fishing permits are not sold within the park.<br>• Specific regulations for fishing on the Current and Jacks Fork in the Ozark National Scenic Riverways can be found on the Missouri Department of Conservation's website …<br><b>Superintendent's Compendium</b> (<a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/ozar/learn/management/ozar-superintendent-s-compendium.htm\">NPS</a>)<br>• Springs – All activities and entry (to include but not limited to fishing, wading, swimming, remote control devices and vessels) into all spring pools and spring branches on federally owned land within ONSR boundaries is prohibited.<br><i>The compendium lists three exceptions to the spring closure: boating access on Big Spring Branch and two vehicle crossings (Cave Spring, Schafer Spring).</i><br><br><b>Statewide: Game Fish Seasons and Limits</b> (p. 20)<br>• Trout · Open Season: All year · Daily Limit: 4<sup>1</sup> · Length Limit: Rainbow: none<sup>1</sup>; Brown: 15\" minimum in streams<br>• Black bass (largemouth, smallmouth, spotted/Kentucky) · Daily Limit: 6<sup>1</sup><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;– From impoundments · Open Season: All year · Length Limit: None<sup>1</sup><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;– From most streams south of the Missouri River<sup>2</sup> · Open Season: May 23, 2026–Feb. 28, 2027 · Length Limit: 12\" minimum<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;– From other streams<sup>2</sup> · Open Season: All year · Length Limit: 12\" minimum<br><sup>1</sup> Applies on all waters, with the exception of those with more specific regulations found on pages 24–35 and on areas that are posted with special regulations.<br><sup>2</sup> Black bass fishing and possession is open year-round on the Mississippi River, all waters north of the south bank of the Missouri River, the St. Francis River downstream from Wappapello Dam, and on streams in that portion of southeast Missouri bounded by a line from Cape Girardeau following Missouri highways 74 and 25, U.S. highways 60, 67, and 160, and the west bank of the Little Black River to the Arkansas state line. In the rest of the state, black bass from streams may not be possessed from March 1–May 22, 2026. Only catch-and-release fishing for black bass is allowed during this time in this area of the state.<br><br><b>Statewide: Nongame Fish Seasons and Limits</b> (p. 21)<br>• Gig, atlatl · Season: Sept. 15–Feb. 15 · Time: Sunrise to midnight · Location: Streams and impounded waters<br><i>Daily limits, and every other method, are in that table on p. 21.</i>",
  goodFlow:null, // TODO: set {min:___,max:___} (CFS) for YOUR good-flow range on this river
  blurb:"The National Park Service says the Current and Jacks Fork rivers “have some of the best sport fishing opportunities in the country for both Smallmouth Bass, Rainbow and Brown Trout” (Ozark National Scenic Riverways). MDC manages the Jacks Fork River from the Hwy. 17 bridge to the Current River (Shannon and Texas counties) as one of its smallmouth bass special management areas, 37.5 miles in its atlas (MDC Fishing Atlas).",
  fish:"smallmouth bass, rainbow and brown trout (NPS, of the Current and Jacks Fork); smallmouth bass (special management area)",
