  parkRegs:"<i>No entry of its own in the digest's Rivers &amp; Streams with Special Rules (pp. 23–25).</i><br><br><b>Standard rule: General Fishing Rules for Rivers &amp; Streams</b> (p. 22)<br>In general, rivers and streams are CLOSED TO ALL FISHING from Oct. 16 through Dec. 31, unless specified for a particular river in the Rivers and Streams with Special Rules section.<br>Fishing through the ice, from the ice, or from any object supported by the ice is prohibited unless specified for a particular river in the Rivers and Streams with Special Rules section.<br>• Black Bass (Smallmouth Bass &amp; Largemouth Bass): January 1 to May 14 · 2 fish daily limit.<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;May 15 to June 15 · Catch and release only. · Artificial lures and flies only.<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;June 16 to October 15 · 5 fish daily limit.<br>• Brook Trout, Brown Trout, Rainbow Trout and their hybrids: January 1 to October 15 · 5 fish or 5 pounds daily limit, whichever is reached first; no length limit. · “Trout” include Brook, Brown, Rainbow, Golden, and all their hybrids.<br>• Landlocked Salmon: April 1 to September 30 · 2 fish daily limit, minimum length 15 inches. · Salmon may not be taken in any water body after September 30 and before April 1. Salmon may not be taken through the ice.<br><i>Other species: the table on p. 22.</i><br>In instances where special limits, seasons, and methods of taking are not noted for a particular river or stream, the General Fishing Rules apply. Rivers and Streams with no closed season refer to General Lakes and Ponds for species not listed.",
  goodFlow:null, // TODO: set {min:___,max:___} (CFS) for YOUR good-flow range on this river
  blurb:"17.6 miles in New Hampshire, drawn from USGS National Hydrography Dataset flowlines. The line stops at each lake's shore (a gap in it is still water): Campton Pond. Runs through Waterville Valley, Thornton, Campton and Livermore. NH Fish & Game's stocked-streams layer has it stocked in Campton, Thornton and Waterville Valley. That layer marks wild brook trout present in Campton, Thornton and Waterville Valley.",
