  blurb:"Lake Sharpe runs about 80 miles down the Missouri from Oahe Dam at Pierre to Big Bend Dam at Fort Thompson. The outline is USGS NHD's, which draws a reservoir at its normal pool.",
  fish:"Walleye must be 15 inches except in July and August, and only one in the daily limit may be 20 inches or longer.",
  regs:"<b>Missouri River exceptions to statewide harvest and length limits</b><br>LAKE SHARPE - Walleye: Minimum length limit 15”, except during July and August when there is no minimum length restriction. Daily limit may include no more than one fish 20” or longer year-round.<br><i>— SD GFP, 2026 South Dakota Fishing Handbook, Missouri River Exceptions to Statewide Harvest and Length Limits, p. 25</i><br><br><b>Inland Missouri River harvest and length limits</b> <i>(daily limit / possession limit)</i><br>• Walleye (additional length restrictions apply to some waters) — 4 (only one 20” or longer) / 8<br>• Northern pike — 6 / 12<br>• Largemouth/smallmouth bass (any combination) — 5 / 10<br>• Yellow perch — 15 / 30<br>• Crappie (any combination) — 15 / 30<br>• Sunfish (bluegill, green, pumpkinseed, redear, orangespotted in any combination) — 15 / 30<br>• Trout/salmon (any combination) — 5 / 10<br>• Muskellunge/tiger muskie — Catch and release only<br>• Rainbow smelt — 5 gallons / No limit<br>• White bass/rock bass* — No limit<br>• Lake herring/lake whitefish — No limit<br>• Bullhead — No limit<br>• Catfish (any combination) — No limit (only one flathead catfish 30” or longer daily)<br>• Sturgeon — Season closed<br>• Paddlefish — Available by special permit only<br>• Rough fish — No limit<br>* The last 5.5 miles of the MR in SD, from Ft. Randall Dam to the SD/NE state line, has a daily limit of 15 and a possession limit of 30 white bass.<br><i>— SD GFP, 2026 South Dakota Fishing Handbook, Missouri River, Inland Missouri River Harvest and Length Limits, p. 27</i><br><br><b>Tribal waters</b><br>Non-tribal members fishing inland waters that overlay Indian trust property need a tribal fishing license. Non-tribal members fishing on the Missouri River and its impoundments need only the state license. However, anglers fishing the Missouri River from tribal lands may need to purchase a tribal fishing license or access permit from the tribe to fish from shore.<br><i>— SD GFP, 2026 South Dakota Fishing Handbook, Tribal Waters, p. 8</i><br><br><b>Game fish spearing/archery seasons and dates</b><br>• Missouri River Inland Waters — May 1 - March 31 — Lake Francis Case, Lake Sharpe, Lake Oahe, On the Missouri River from Ft. Randall Dam downstream 5.5 miles to the Nebraska border — All game fish species except paddlefish, muskie, and sturgeon<br><i>— SD GFP, 2026 South Dakota Fishing Handbook, Spearing and Archery, p. 51</i><br><br><b>Boating restrictions</b><br>STANLEY COUNTY: No boating zone on Lake Sharpe south of the Lake Oahe Powerhouse from the “T-Dike” south to a signed point 2,100’ downstream inside the buoys; the waters inside Oahe Marina are a no wake zone; the waters from the mouth of the Bad River upstream to the pedestrian footbridge are a no wake zone.<br><i>— SD GFP, 2026 South Dakota Fishing Handbook, Boating Restrictions, p. 43</i><br><br><b>Aquatic invasive species infested waters</b><br>• LAKE SHARPE — Zebra Mussel, Asian Clam, Rudd, Pondweed, Eurasian Watermilfoil, Purple Loosestrife, Common Reed<br>For a complete list of AIS regulations and the most current list of AIS infested waters, visit SDLEASTWANTED.SD.GOV.<br><i>— SD GFP, 2026 South Dakota Fishing Handbook, Aquatic Invasive Species Infested Waters, p. 61</i>",
  regsNote:"From South Dakota Game, Fish and Parks' <b>2026 South Dakota Fishing Handbook</b>: the lake's own Missouri River exception, then the inland Missouri River limits, the tribal-waters rule and the lines that name the lake, word for word with pages. The handbook's general rules (bait, lines and hooks, spearing and archery, set-lines, boating) aren't reproduced, except lines in them that name this lake. Read the current handbook before you fish.",
